{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NZmvE6VofwE11d67GoFfRz","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Here's the complete file contents:\n\nThe test file shows an interesting approach to handling the problem you mentioned. Here's how it addresses the sequential execution issue:\n\n**Key solution: Separate temporary directories per binary**\n\nThe critical function is `runRemovalTest()` (lines 124-141), which handles file-mutating operations like unlink:\n\n- **Line 129-130**: Creates two separate `TempDir()` instances (`goDir` and `refDir`)\n- **Line 131-132**: Calls the setup function independently for each directory, creating identical initial state\n- **Line 133-134**: Runs each binary in its own separate working directory\n- **Line 137-140**: Verifies the file was actually removed (post-condition check)\n\nThis avoids the sequential execution problem entirely: the Go implementation (`goBin`) runs first in `goDir` and deletes the file there, then the reference binary (`refBin`) runs in its own `refDir` and also gets to delete the file.\n\n**Tests that don't mutate files** use `RunDiffTests()` (lines 52-115) for:\n- Error cases (zero args, extra operands, nonexistent files, directories)\n- Help/version flags\n\nThese stateless tests safely run both binaries in the same working directory sequentially because they don't modify filesystem state.\n\n**Result comparison**: The `compareResults()` function (lines 169-189) normalizes binary names in output before comparing exit codes and stdout/stderr, ensuring the two implementations match behavior exactly."}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8047bac1-fb35-41cf-b5eb-0ce8a2f0ef17","uuid":"2d2cee36-9143-49c8-9c72-26273a4520eb","timestamp":"2026-05-23T12:36:27.245Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"Find and read `cmd/unlink/unlink_test.go` in the repo at /private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/5529/. I nee