o the link command.\n\nNow I'm planning out the test structure — I'll follow the same pattern as the unlink test, creating separate temp directories for both binaries, then running each with a source file and target file to verify the hard link is created correctly with matching inodes. I need to cover the success case plus error cases for zero arguments, one argument, three arguments, and a nonexistent source file.\n\nWriting the test file... Writing test cases... Writing test cases... Writing test helper functions... I'm checking the error message formats between our binary and GNU glink to ensure they match properly—our implementation uses \"link\" as the program name while glink uses \"glink\", and I need to normalize these differences in the test comparison.\n\nThe regex pattern should correctly handle the \"Try 'glink --help'\" case by matching \"glink\" and replacing it with \"link\", while leaving other instances of \"link\" in phrases like \"cannot create link\" untouched since they're not preceded by the optional path component. with \"link\", so no change. For glink, the \"glink:\" prefix becomes \"link:\" in the normalized output. Since both the Go and reference implementations get normalized the same way, any edge cases like \"link\" appearing in file paths or within words like \"blink\" would affect both equally, so the comparison remains valid.\n\nNow I'm verifying the hard link with `os.SameFile`, which compares the inode and device information from `os.Stat` to confirm both paths point to the same file — that's the cleanest approach for this test.\n\nWriting the test file...","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2308,"cache_read_input_tokens":24789,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2308},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"pare